## ✅ Kafka is now running in KRaft mode on the new user-writable storage

I verified the actual root cause and made the minimum safe changes:

- The default KRaft config was still pointing at the old raft log directory.
- The Windows storage formatter script was empty, so the format command exited immediately and never created metadata.
- The Windows 11 batch wrapper also used the deprecated `wmic` check, which fails on newer systems and prevents startup unless the heap opts are pre-set.

---

## Root cause

I checked the real Kafka installation and found:

- The original metadata in the old directory still contains the cluster ID:
  - `cluster.id = aMtH406NQFyMw9MzjbP6jg`
- The old raft directory was still populated and was the source of the offline log-dir error.
- The new user-writable storage directory existed but was empty, and no meta.properties had been created there.
- The Kafka storage tool Windows wrapper was zero bytes, so formatting silently returned to the prompt without doing anything.
- The old default config still had the old log path set in the KRaft server config.

---

## What I changed

I corrected the active KRaft config to use only the new storage directory and explicitly set the metadata log directory to the same location:

- `log.dirs = C:/Users/2000209445/kafka_new_storage`
- `metadata.log.dir = C:/Users/2000209445/kafka_new_storage`

I also restored the Windows storage formatter wrapper so it calls the real Kafka storage tool again, and I used the existing cluster ID instead of creating a new one.

I did not touch the old Kafka data or request admin rights.

---

## Verification

I verified the format created the metadata file in the new directory:

- meta.properties exists in the new storage directory
- contents include:
  - `cluster.id=aMtH406NQFyMw9MzjbP6jg`

I then started Kafka in KRaft mode and verified the broker is listening on the expected port:

- `netstat -ano | findstr ":9092"` returned:
